Summary/Abstract.
UK SMEs come under pressure to get Cyber Essentials certification to obtain public sector
contracts, cyber insurance and supply chain compliance. However, challenges exist faced by
SMEs such as lack of cybersecurity experience, small budgets (consultancy costs between
£1,500-£5,000) and understanding technical requirements. This project is the creation of the
Tanasiom Aegis Cyber Essentials Readiness Toolkit—the first structured, user-friendly
solution designed to support UK SMEs’ self-initiating preparation for certification.
The toolkit consists of a self-assessing questionnaire that includes a set of 50 questions that
is used to select five core Cyber Essentials control areas, automating scoring according to
gaps analysed, template forms with recommendations for policy and implementation, and a
prototype dashboard created in React. The project employs design-based research
methodology which merges literature analysis with pragmatic artefact development.
For evidence-based assessment through an initial simulated case study (18 workers working
in a professional services firm), early results confirm measurable improvement, as
demonstrated by an organisational readiness profile that went from 42% at baseline to 92%
since remediation, mostly through improvements in configuration and documenting changes
in policy documents. Background study, questionnaire generation, template generation of
templates and dashboard prototyping were added in the interim phase. Fina l semester is
about completing the functionality of artefact functionality, final evaluation, holistic
evaluation and final reports preparation.
