Chapter 1: Introduction
1.1Project Topic and Rationale
The UK Government’s Cyber Essentials (CE) scheme provides a baseline level of cybersecurity
protection, which safeguards against approximately 80% of common cyber-attacks, as set out by the
National Cyber Security Centre for the UK Government (NCSC, 2024). When SMEs compete for a
government contract, deal with sensitive customer data, or are required to comply with cyber
insurance requirements, they need to demonstrate compliance. What started as a voluntary best
practice has become a necessity for SMEs in government supply chains. Yet SMEs face structural
implementation barriers. According to study findings, 47 percent find it difficult to apply security
measures despite awareness of the threat, attributed mainly to skills shortages, financial constraints,
and lack of dedicated cybersecurity expertise (IDC, 2023).
Security responsibilities are usually passed down to non-specialist staff, such as directors, office
managers, and operations personnel, who do not have a technical background. Costs of external
consultancy (£1,500-£5,000) are significant unplanned expenses for businesses with tight margins
(Small Business Commissioner, 2023).
These issues are followed by unsuccessful tender applications for procurement, compliance gaps,
regulatory fines, increases in insurance costs, and impact to the business's reputation. The project
introduces the Tanasiom Aegis Cyber Essentials Readiness Toolkit based on the UK SME
requirements, capabilities, and constraints. Market validation with LinkedIn engagement with SME
decision-makers confirmed frustration with existing guidance and the demand for structured
approaches. Other regulatory insights were gained while completing Clifford Chance LLP Cyber
Security Virtual Internship (December 2023) on ICO procedures and GDPR breach response, which
shaped toolkit design.
1.2 Project Aims and Objectives
Aim: Create, analyse, and evaluate a Cyber Essentials Readiness Toolkit that will support UK SMEs to
understand, prepare for and achieve certification efficiently and cost-effectively, reducing reliance
on external consultancy while supporting informed decision-making and sustainable compliance.
Objectives:
1. Investigate CE requirements and SME security challenges by looking at NCSC/IASME guidance via a
rigorous structured review of academic literature and industry reports.
2. Establish a structured readiness assessment by connecting five CE control areas linking technical
requirements to business-accessible actions.
3. Build a pragmatic toolkit containing a 50-question questionnaire, templates, policy documentation,
and a dashboard prototype.
4. Provide an action plan for remediation, gap prioritisation, and support for evidence preparation.
5. Evaluate the toolkit using a simulated SME case study showing measurable improvement in
readiness.
6. Consider legal, ethical, and professional aspects, including GDPR conformity and compliance with
BCS Code of Conduct.
This project addresses the BSc Computer Networking & Cyber Security learning outcomes of
applying the cybersecurity framework, working in software development, understanding compliance
regulations, professional responsibility, and project management skills.
1.3 Methodology
The Project adapts a design science research methodology as a basis for research into information
systems development in the face of practical problems (Hevner et al., 2004). It involves cyclical
design, development, and evaluation leading to artefacts to fill identified gaps. The research takes a
pragmatic philosophical worldview emphasizing real-world applicability.
Project Phases:
PHASE 1 (October-November 2025): Review of SME cybersecurity difficulties and CE requirements,
validation via LinkedIn engagement with 8 SME decision makers in the market.
Phase 2 (November – December 2025): Functional requirements specification & information
architecture for five CE control domains + Scoring methodology designing + Dashboard wireframe
design.
Phase 3 (December 2025 - January 2026): 50-question questionnaire, 7 policy templates, Excel
scoring matrix with automated calculations, and React dashboard prototype using
Vite/Bootstrap/Chart.js.
Phase 4 (January 2026): Simulated SME profile development (18 employees, professional service
firm, Microsoft 365 environment without existing policies), baseline preparedness scoring,
remediation modelling, post-remediation evaluation.
Phase 5 (January-March 2026): Evaluation analysis, legal/ethical/professional considerations review,
final report preparation, viva voce preparation.
Data sources refer to reliable secondary resources (NCSC, IASME, academic literature) validated with
simulated case study. No personal participant data were collected - adhering to ethical standards.
