Chapter 2: Background Research
2.1 A literature review of Related Work.
External Compliance Pressure. Cyber Essentials certification became a formal requirement rather
than voluntary for UK SMEs. NCSC (2024) confirms certification is compulsory for those making
contracts which incorporate sensitive government data or technical services provision. This goes well
beyond government procurement into commercial supply chains, as larger organizations require
supplier certification, thus creating compliance pyramids across markets. Baseline control
verification is required for cybersecurity insurance companies; some insurers require CE on policy
issuance by the insurers (Beazley, 2023).
Affected organisations get approximately 10 percent premium reduction through certification.
Furthermore, CE compliance shows the right level of technical and organisational steps within the
framework of GDPR Article 32 and may limit ICO exposure in a breach investigation. Both the EU
NIS2 Directive and future UK supply chain regulations are expanding mandatory cybersecurity
measures (BCS, 2023).
Restrictions on the Resources and the Resources and Skills shortage. Systemic barriers to the
implementation are faced by SMEs despite the increased focus on the certification despite the
growing demand for certification. According to empirical evidence, SMEs generally do not have
specific cybersecurity expertise, and the duties of security are also shared in the common hands of
the non-technical staff (IDC, 2023). Roughly 47 per cent face major challenges with adopting security
technology, which are driven by skills shortages and a lack of internal capacity. Skills gaps show up as
the inability to interpret technical requirements, the uncertain nature of controls that relate to IT
environments (most notably cloud and BYOD), the inability to configure systems without support,
and confusion about what evidence is required.
Von Solms and Van Niekerk (2013) state this, they believe information security governance
frameworks assume organizational maturity, which many small organisations lack, leading to
fundamental misalignments between standards and capacities. External consultancy costs £1,500 –
£5,000 depending on complexity (Small Business Commissioner, 2023), with very large, unplanned
costs competing with revenue stream activities.
Certification assessment fees (£300 basic, £500+ Plus) put extra pressure. SME cyber maturity is
hampered by time and cognitive load; senior decision-makers have extensive and overlapping
operational and compliance responsibilities (NCSC, 2024), meaning they have relatively little
capacity to deploy technical standards. According to Dawson (2024), compliance effort stalls due to
competing priorities and next steps that are unclear. Such a phenomenon generates a paradox
where SMEs know compliance is critical but are unable to invest resources. Technical Complexity.
SMEs face difficulty understanding whether the CE requirements are relevant to current IT
applications practice. NCSC (2024) notes BYOD, cloud services and remote work is making definition
of scope definition for assessment difficult. Cloud duty are still a significant source of confusions. The
cloud remains a big reason for doubt as to who is responsible for it. According to NCSC (2024), all
regulations are the responsibility of organisations, regardless of managing external infrastructure.
However, understanding the responsibility for IaaS versus PaaS versus SaaS environments requires
technical knowledge that many SME decision stakeholders do not have.
The IASME certification program encourages organisations to confirm compliance in detail for all inscope systems themselves, placing burden on those who lack such technical assurance to review
firewall configurations, patch management procedures, or strict configuration rules. Accordingly,
SMEs may misinterpret requirements, wrong-define scope or utilize partial controls that may look
sufficient but are not in keeping with certification. Assessment Pressure. This failed certification
affects more than money. For public sector type work in SMEs, failure to achieve certification has a
direct impact on revenue opportunities. Small Business Commissioner (2023) demonstrated that
SMEs are heavily worried about the certification process, especially when examination deadlines are
the same as the time submission comes around the final phase of a contract. CE test is binary
(Pass/Fail) with little partial credit. There are even single high-level failures – a lack of multi-factor
authentication or insufficient evidence about patch management – that could ultimately lead to
failure despite the proper application of most of the controls.
2.2 Products/Solutions Other Products/Solutions in Relation for Review.
The NCSC (2024) gives detailed requirements documentation for CE. These technical requirements
with regular update are all accurately outlined in authoritative sources. And yet the documentation
is more descriptive than instructional — describing the controls that need to be in place, but not
granular enough about how SMEs implement controls in resource-starved contexts. Content
assumes technical capacity that is absent for many SME practitioners.
Commercial Managed service providers -- there is often CE prep in companies that provide CE prep
as a package or a standalone consulting operation. These SME constraints are serious problems.
First, the cost for some services, which are too often non-transparent in terms of cost, is out of sight.
It indicates SMEs develop dependence on external providers, decreasing self-awareness and longterm sustainability (IACIS, 2024). The MSP processes present “black box” situations in which SMEs
take instructions that they are not sure of and they don’t understand the reason behind any of them,
which contradicts board-level accountability frameworks. Enterprise compliance: Commercial
business process control (GRC) products (OneTrust, ServiceNow, RSA Archer) offer holistic
compliance services designed for large companies that have highly capable individuals and teams to
oversee it. These are out of place for SMEs due to cost (usually > £10,000 per year), the complexity
of their features, and the need to train them directly from start to finish, for which the small
business cannot afford.
The majority of commercial tools do automated scanning or checkbox compliance that gives little to
no context about why controls need to matter, encouraging compliance-by-checkbox rather than
security gains. Gap Analysis: On comparative analysis the difference is that authoritative advice and
commercial expertise have their merits but neither offers meaningful SME support in the process of
transferring CE stipulations into practice via the delivery of well-planned, transparent, sustainable
processes. The new toolkit addresses this gap and creates: SME specific vs generic guidance, low
cost- self-service vs expensive consultancy, step-by-step implementation guidance versus high level
requirements, transparent explanation vs technical buzzwords, building internal skills vs dependency
creating services, built in evidence preparation support, sustenance for annual renewal vs one off
engagement.
