Chapter 3: Requirements Analysis and Specification
3.1 Problem Analysis
CE certification among UK SMEs is required for public sector contracts, customer assurance, and
cyber insurance compliance. Nevertheless, there remains a persistent implementation gap
characterised by: Interpretation Uncertainty (SME decision-makers find it challenging to translate
technical CE requirements into concrete action such as defining scope/cloud responsibility/evidence
requirements); Resource Constraints (the majority of SMEs do not have dedicated cybersecurity staff
and cannot justify costs of £1,500-£5,000 for external consultancy); Process Fragmentation (the
current guidance lists requirements without implementation processes); Assessment Anxiety (the
fear of certification failure resulting from hidden requirements leads non-technical members of the
team to feel anxious); Sustainability Challenges (even externally-assisted SMEs often lack internal
knowledge to maintain controls for their annual renewal).
Problem Statement: We have no systematic, easily accessible readiness processes tailored to a
specific group, the SME community; they enable non-technical executives to prepare with purpose,
confidence and at a minimum, external dependencies. This problem is substantiated by evidence
from academic literature demonstrating skills and resource deficits (IDC, 2023; Von Solms and Van
Niekerk, 2013), government recognition of challenges of SME implementation (NCSC, 2024), market
validation of the demand for self-service guidance, and commercial evidence of the cost of
consultancy posing access barriers (Small Business Commissioner, 2023).
