Chapter 4: Software Design
The chapter describes the software design of the Tanasiom Aegis Cyber Essentials Readiness
Toolkit, covering approximately 50% of the complete system design as required for the
interim submission. This design includes user interface mockups, data storage s chema, and
software architecture components.
4.1 User-Interface Design.
Clear, simple, progressive disclosure is fundamental to UI design. Highlights and comments at the
front should serve as navigation points. Non-technical users will see little friction in accessing rich
guidance where needed. Dashboard Home Screen: Clear, minimal design with Tanasiom Aegis
branding, short purpose explanation, "Start Assessment" call-to-action button prominently placed.
Secondary navigation has “About Cyber Essentials” educational resources and “Load Saved
Assessment” functionality. One strategy is designed to prevent overwhelming first-time users with
easy-to-navigate guides and quizzes but have optional educational elements. Assessment Question
Interface: Showing a single question at a time with a bar displaying the progress of "Question X of 50
(Y% complete)." Text: the question itself is written in the common language; there's also an optional
"More Info" expandable panel for technical questions. Four big, clearly labeled “response buttons”
(YES/PARTIAL/NO/NOT APPLICABLE) arrayed vertically. Navigation buttons (Previous/Next) at the
bottom. Cognitive overload is minimized one question at a time. Big buttons for responding on
touch-responsive mobile devices. Optional “More Info” panel is the second version of progressive
disclosure, necessary information is shown at once and some context is presented for those who are
interested in elaborating. Dashboard: Separated into 3 panels. Top panel presents total readiness
score as large percentage with coloured classification (72% - Nearly Ready in amber). Central panel
presents radar chart with 5 axes representing CE control area with a score between 0 and 100%. In
the bottom panel, we have prioritised gaps with severity indicators (Critical/High/Medium/Low) and
"View Guidance" buttons. A visual hierarchy displays board-level overview first, then technical
breakdown. Radar chart indicates at a glance strengths/weaknesses. Colour coded severity
indicators take advantage of common conventions. ”View Guidance” buttons allow for a transparent
movement from where you can find a problem to implementing a solution.
More screens illustrate steps in remediation on detailed pathways and step-by-step instructions,
and templates available for download/preview: template library, accessibility in PDF gap analysis
report export, assessment history (e.g., progress by time).
