4.3 Software Architecture:
Toolkit follows a modular and component-based architecture which is divided into three tiers.
Presentation Layer (React Components): UI rendering, user input capture, visual feedback. Key
Components: App.jsx (root component managing state and routing), HomePage.jsx (landing screen),
AssessmentQuestion.jsx (question display with response capture), ProgressTracker.jsx (visual
progress indicator), ResultsDashboard.jsx (overall results visualisation), RadarChart.jsx (Chart.js
wrapper), GapList.jsx (prioritised gap display), TemplateLibrary.jsx (template browser and
download), PDFExporter.jsx (report generation using jsPDF integration). Business Logic Layer
(JavaScript Modules): For scoring calculations, gap analysis logic, data transformation.
As isolated pure JS functions as well, apart from UI components, ensure testability and reusability.
Key Modules: scoringEngine.js (measures total and per-control scores), gapAnalyser.js (identifies
failed requirements and severity), questionMappings.js (maps questions to CE requirements),
remediationGuidance.js (provides contextual recommendations), dataValidator.js (ensures response
data integrity). Data Layer (localStorage/Future API): abstraction layer providing save/load/delete
methods, serialisation for JavaScript objects into JSON and a simple key-value user interface to hide
the storage mechanism from business logic will be used.
Future Migration: Replace storageService.js with HTTP client, and add JWT authentication, serverside validation. No logic in the business or presentation layer updated for the abstraction.
Interaction Flow between Components: (1) User clicks response button in AssessmentQuestion
component; (2) Response data bubbles up into App.jsx via callback; (3) App.jsx with useState to
update assessment state; (4) App.jsx invokes scoringEngine.calculateScores(); (5) Scoring result in
state and passed to ResultsDashboard; (6) ResultsDashboard hands off data to child components; (7)
User interaction triggers TemplateLibrary to display relevant templates. For simpler academic use
and viva demonstration in which we use React's built-in capabilities rather than using an external
library. Data on evaluation in App.jsx with useState, but passed on to child elements thanks to props,
also following the unidirectional data flow of React. Scoring Engine Algorithm: The scoring engine
converts responses of questionnaires into control-level scores, overall readiness percentage and
readiness classification depending on CE assessment method. Algorithm employs weighted scoring
with binding failure conditions to guarantee failure with the IASME certifiction's binary pass/fail
structure. All questions contribute two points max (Yes=2, Partial=1, No=0).
Not Applicable responses removed from scoring denominator to avoid penalizing organisations for
legitimately irrelevant controls. Handling critical failure: If any CE requirement goes wrong (no multifactor authentication for administrative access, unmanaged security updates), assessment is
automatically classified as 'Not Ready' regardless of total percentage score derived. It mirrors CE
evaluations in the field when vital control failures result in certification failure even as high scores in
other aspects avoid producing deceptive “false readiness” scores and give SMEs true feedback on
their compliance posture.
