Bootstrap made UI development faster to keep responsive. localStorage: Although it simplifies MVP
deployments, localStorage has limitations in data persistence and cross-device access—fine for
academic testing but backend storage is critical for commercialization.
The complex grading algorithm implementation revealed complexity that needed careful edge case
testing.
Research Method: Design-based research offered a suitable approach that was beneficial for
balancing the creation of working artefact of course with its academic contribution. Iterative designdev-evaluate cycles enabled refinement based upon new insights. Meanwhile, simulated evaluation
creates restrictions to generalisability, as real-world pilot testing with actual SMEs would greatly
improve findings validity. LinkedIn outreach generated valuable responses that guided design
decisions, however, the small sample size (n=5 responses from n=8 contacts) may have resulted in
biased representation based on self-selection.
Legal, social, ethical, professional issues: Clifford Chance internship furthered understanding of UK
data protection law, supporting GDPR alignment areas in toolkit based on being exposed to ICO
enforcement processes.
Toolkit comes with disclaimers that it provides guidance not legal advice. All policy templates are our
work and avoid copyright concerns. The simulated evaluation method met ethical requirements to
protect important security information on sensitive data. This is good for ethics but this limits realworld applicability. If studies on human subjects were conducted in future they would obviously
have to be approved or obtain strong ethical approval and informed consent. Project is based on BCS
Professional Principles: Public interest (enabling affordable cybersecurity guidance for SMEs in the
UK benefits UK cyber resilience), professional competence (exhaustive research proves
technical/analytical capabilities), duty to relevant authority (complying with NCSC/IASME guidelines
ensures authoritative guidance), duty to profession (transparent methodology and ethical research
practices). Toolkit tackles digital inequality by mitigating barriers to cybersecurity compliance for
organisations with limited resources. But “accessible” guidance remains predicated on basic digital
literacy and proficiency in English language. Next versions will need to consider accessibility towards
people with disabilities and multi-language support.
Project Management – keeping the boundaries of the scope clear proved tough especially when you
find some really interesting similar subjects. Defining “out of scope” clearly was good for staving off
scope-creep. The external validation from regular supervisor reviews helped. Maintaining my time
for final year project work, other modules and job alongside personal schedule had its challenges as
well. Specific breakdowns of work and accurate estimates of a timeline helped to avoid freezes. But
unexpected technical problems took longer than planned, underlining the need for buffer time. A lot
of development documentation made interim reporting a lot easier to do.
